00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,480
Exercise 2-6. Consonant-consonant liaisons. I just didn't get the chance.

2
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:12,820
I've been late twice. In the preceding examples, you can see that because the

3
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:19,460
ending st of just and the beginning d of didn't are so near each other in the

4
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:24,060
mouth, it's not worth the effort to start the sound all over again, so they just

5
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:30,680
flow into each other. You don't say, I just didn't get the chance, but do say, I

6
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:36,380
just didn't get the chance. In the same way, it's too much work to say, I've been

7
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:41,720
late twice. So you say it almost as if it were a single word, I've been late twice.

8
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:48,860
The sound of th is a special case. It's a floater between areas. The sound is

9
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:53,200
sometimes created by the tongue popping out from between the teeth and other on

10
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:58,260
the back of the top teeth, combining with various letters to form a new composite

11
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:05,140
sound. For instance, S moves forward and the F moves back to meet at the midpoint

12
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:09,940
between the two. Note, each of the categories in the drawing contains two

13
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:14,960
labels, voiced and unvoiced. What does that mean? Put your thumb and index

14
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:20,140
fingers on your throat and say ZZZ. You should feel a vibration from your throat

15
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:26,480
in your fingers. If you whisper that same sound, you end up with ss and you feel

16
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:33,400
that your fingers don't vibrate. So, zz is a voiced sound, ss unvoiced. The

17
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:48,140
consonants in the two left columns are paired like that. B P D T V P G C G G Z Z

18
00:01:48,140 --> 00:02:01,440
Z Z Z Z Z Z Z Z Z er, mmm, mmm, mmm, yeh, wah.
